      "topic": "Hyperthyroidism",
      "category": "Endocrine & Metabolic",
      "question": {
        "scenario": "A 35-year-old female presents with a palpable goiter and complains of weight gain. Her thyroid function tests are normal (FTI 4.2 mIU/L), TSH 0.9 mIU/L, but free T4 is elevated at 7.2 mcg/dL.",
        "stem": "What is the most appropriate next step in differential diagnosis for this patient?",
        "options": {
          "A": "Thyroid hormone replacement therapy (THTR)",
          "B": "Differentiated thyroiditis, possibly due to radiation or iodine uptake",
          "C": "Evaluation for Cushing's syndrome",
          "D": "Phebitism evaluation"
        }
      },
      "correct_answer": "B",
      "explanation": "The normal TSH and FTI suggest hyperthyroidism rather than autoimmune conditions. Elevated free T4 with these labs points towards differentiated thyroiditis, likely due to radiation or iodine exposure.",
